{"cuisine/tlingit-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/tlingit-cuisine","n":"Tlingit cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Alaska Native foodways","cuisine/alaska-native-foodways"]],"sub":"Indigenous foodways, Southeast Alaska","names":"Lingít","intro":[["The Tlingit live along Southeast Alaska's coast and islands, where food comes from both the forest and the sea. ",["Salmon","ingredient/salmon"]," is the center of the year, smoked and dried for winter. In spring, ",["Herring","ingredient/herring"]," eggs are gathered on hemlock branches or kelp, and ",["Eulachon","ingredient/eulachon"]," are rendered into a rich oil."]],"src":"note","sources":[{"name":"Alaska Native Heritage Center","url":"https://www.alaskanative.net/"},{"name":"University of Alaska Museum of the North, Alaska Native cultures guide","url":"https://uaf.edu/museum/education/docents/pdfs/NC2-3_Intro_2014.pdf"}],"actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"tlingit-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tlingit_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Alaska Native foodways","cuisine/alaska-native-foodways"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Alaska Native foodways","cuisine/alaska-native-foodways"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas"},"cuisine/cuisine-of-the-pennsylvania-dutch":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/cuisine-of-the-pennsylvania-dutch","n":"Cuisine of the Pennsylvania Dutch","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Cuisine of the Mid-Atlantic United States","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-mid-atlantic-united-states"],["Fusion cuisine","cuisine/fusion-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in United States of America","intro":[["Pennsylvania Dutch cuisine is the typical and traditional fare of the Pennsylvania Dutch."],["Pennsylvania Dutch cuisine reflects influences of the Pennsylvania Dutch's German heritage, agrarian society, and rejection of rapid change."],["It is common to find Pennsylvania Dutch cuisine throughout the Philadelphia, Allentown, and Lancaster regions of Pennsylvania."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"cuisine-of-the-pennsylvania-dutch"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuisine_of_the_Pennsylvania_Dutch"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":2,"links":[["Fasnacht (doughnut)","dish/fasnacht-doughnut"],["Lebanon bologna","dish/lebanon-bologna"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Cuisine of the Mid-Atlantic United States","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-mid-atlantic-united-states"],["Fusion cuisine","cuisine/fusion-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Pennsylvania Dutch Country Cooking","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"William Woys Weaver","note":"Traditional Pennsylvania Dutch recipes from a food historian of the region.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9781558595682","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781558595682"},{"name":"As American as Shoofly Pie: The Foodlore and Fakelore of Pennsylvania Dutch Cuisine","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"William Woys Weaver","note":"Food history of Pennsylvania Dutch cooking with recipes, published by the University of Pennsylvania Press.","url":"https://www.degruyterbrill.com/document/doi/10.9783/9780812207712/html","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Beef","ingredient/beef"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Cuisine of the Mid-Atlantic United States","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-mid-atlantic-united-states"],["Fusion cuisine","cuisine/fusion-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/d/db/US_supermarket_fasnacht_pastries%2C_rectangular%2C_Feb_2013.jpg/500px-US_supermarket_fasnacht_pastries%2C_rectangular%2C_Feb_2013.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Jim10701","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:US_supermarket_fasnacht_pastries%2C_rectangular%2C_Feb_2013.jpg","of":"Fasnacht (doughnut)"}},"cuisine/north-karnataka":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/north-karnataka","n":"Uttara Karnataka cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Karnataka cuisine","cuisine/karnataka-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in India","intro":[["North Karnataka,  is a geographical region in Deccan Plateau from 300 to 730 metres (980 to 2,400 ft) elevation that constitutes the north region of the Karnataka state in India and the region consists of 14 districts. It is drained by the Krishna River and its tributaries the Bhima, Ghataprabha, Malaprabha, and Tungabhadra. North Karnataka lies within the Deccan thorn scrub forests ecoregion, which extends north into eastern Maharashtra."],["North Karnataka consists of total 14 districts and comprises the regions known as (Kalyan-Karnataka) – Kalaburagi division  and (Kittur-Karnataka) – Belagavi division. It includes districts of Bagalkote, Bijapur, Gadag, Dharwad, Haveri, Uttara kannada, Belagavi, Bellary, Bidar, Kalaburagi, Koppal, Raichur, Vijayanagara, Yadgir."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"north-karnataka"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Karnataka"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Karnataka cuisine","cuisine/karnataka-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Karnataka cuisine","cuisine/karnataka-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"ingredient/rapini":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/rapini","n":"Rapini","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 3 cuisines","intro":[["Rapini is a green cruciferous vegetable, with the leaves, buds, and stems all being edible; the buds somewhat resemble broccoli. Rapini is known for its bitter taste, and is particularly associated with Mediterranean cuisine. It is a particularly rich dietary source of vitamin K."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-rapini"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rapini"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["Galician cuisine","cuisine/galician-cuisine",[["Caldo galego","dish/caldo-galego"]]],["Portuguese cuisine","cuisine/portuguese-cuisine",[["Bacalhau à minhota","dish/bacalhau-a-minhota"]]],["Spanish cuisine","cuisine/spanish-cuisine",[["Caldo galego","dish/caldo-galego"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Bacalhau","ingredient/bacalhau"],["Fried potatoes","ingredient/fried-potatoes"],["Fries","ingredient/fries"],["Grelos","ingredient/grelos"],["Olive","ingredient/olive"],["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Olives","ingredient/olives"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Salt cod","ingredient/salt-cod"],["Bacon","ingredient/bacon"],["Cabbage","ingredient/cabbage"],["Chorizo","ingredient/chorizo"],["Lacón","ingredient/lacon"],["Phaseolus vulgaris","ingredient/phaseolus-vulgaris"],["Pig fat","ingredient/pig-fat"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/7/76/Rapini.jpg","w":500,"h":567,"by":"Roedelius","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rapini.jpg"}},"dish/bichak":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/bichak","n":"Bichak","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Uzbek cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Bichak is a stuffed baked or fried pastry that comes in different forms (round, triangle, quadrangle) appetizer or meal similar to a turnover, served in Central Asia cuisines including Uzbek cuisine, Tajik cuisine, Afghan cuisine, and Middle Eastern cuisine, most notably in Moroccan cuisine. It is often served during tea or coffee hour. Bichak can be stuffed with pumpkin, veggies, and jam for a sweet taste, or meat and cheese for a savory addition to lunch.  Bichak is also popular because it can be prepared in large quantities."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-bichak"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bichak"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Uzbek cuisine","cuisine/uzbek-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]},{"h":"More from Uzbek cuisine","links":[["Beshbarmak","dish/beshbarmak"],["Boortsog","dish/boortsog"],["Chorba","dish/chorba"],["Dimlama","dish/dimlama"],["Dovga","dish/dovga"],["Kuurdak","dish/kuurdak"],["Mastava","dish/mastava"],["Naryn (dish)","dish/naryn-dish"],["Nisholda","dish/nisholda"],["Orama (dish)","dish/orama-dish"],["Pilaf","dish/pilaf"],["Qazı","dish/qaz"],["Samarkand non","dish/samarkand-non"],["Shakarap","dish/shakarap"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/c/c5/%D0%91%D0%B8%D1%87%D0%B0%D0%BA_%D0%B8%D0%B7_%D1%82%D1%8B%D0%BA%D0%B2%D1%8B.jpg/500px-%D0%91%D0%B8%D1%87%D0%B0%D0%BA_%D0%B8%D0%B7_%D1%82%D1%8B%D0%BA%D0%B2%D1%8B.jpg","w":500,"h":281,"by":"Defrelic","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%91%D0%B8%D1%87%D0%B0%D0%BA_%D0%B8%D0%B7_%D1%82%D1%8B%D0%BA%D0%B2%D1%8B.jpg"}},"ingredient/papaver":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/papaver","n":"Papaver","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Papaver  is a genus of 70–100 species of frost-tolerant annuals, biennials, and perennials native to temperate and cold regions of Eurasia, Africa and North America. It is the type genus of the poppy family, Papaveraceae."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-papaver"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Papaver"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Jewish cuisine","cuisine/jewish-cuisine",[["Cebularz","dish/cebularz"],["Hamantash","dish/hamantash"]]],["Polish cuisine","cuisine/polish-cuisine",[["Cebularz","dish/cebularz"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Wheat","ingredient/wheat"],["Yeast","ingredient/yeast"],["Poppy seed","ingredient/poppy-seed"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/e/ed/Papaver_April_2010-3.jpg/500px-Papaver_April_2010-3.jpg","w":500,"h":471,"by":"Alvesgaspar","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Papaver_April_2010-3.jpg"}},"dish/conch-fritter":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/conch-fritter","n":"Conch fritter","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Bahamian cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Conch is any of a number of different medium-to-large-sized sea snails. Conch shells typically have a high spire and a noticeable siphonal canal (in other words, the shell comes to a noticeable point on both ends)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-conch-fritter"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conch_fritter"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Bahamian cuisine","cuisine/bahamian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Conch","ingredient/conch"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/7/75/Conch_fritters_Mango_Festival_2011.jpg/500px-Conch_fritters_Mango_Festival_2011.jpg","w":500,"h":335,"by":"Bob B. Brown","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Conch_fritters_Mango_Festival_2011.jpg"}},"dish/javanese-doughnut":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/javanese-doughnut","n":"Javanese doughnut","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Javanese cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["The Javanese doughnut, also known in Indonesian as donat jawa, is a traditional doughnut snack, typically savoury, made of cassava instead of potato or flour. This doughnut is quite popular in Javanese cuisine in Java, especially in Central Java, Yogyakarta, and East Java, Indonesia. It is usually served during Javanese occasions, such as wedding parties, wayang shows, ketoprak theater, and Ramadan."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-javanese-doughnut"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Javanese_doughnut"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Javanese cuisine","cuisine/javanese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Cassava","ingredient/cassava"]]},{"h":"More from Javanese cuisine","links":[["Buntil","dish/buntil"],["Getuk goreng","dish/getuk-goreng"],["Megono","dish/megono"],["Nasi ambeng","dish/nasi-ambeng"],["Nasi gandul","dish/nasi-gandul"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/c/cb/Donat_jawa.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Rahmawati Hasanah","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Donat_jawa.jpg"}},"ingredient/piper-sarmentosum":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/piper-sarmentosum","n":"Piper sarmentosum","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Piper sarmentosum (lolot pepper, lolot, wild betel) is a plant in the family Piperaceae used in many Southeast Asian cuisines. The leaves are often confused with betel, but they lack the intense taste of the betel leaves and are significantly smaller."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-piper-sarmentosum"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piper_sarmentosum"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Thai cuisine","cuisine/thai-cuisine",[["Miang kham","dish/miang-kham"]]]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/b/b6/Piper_sarmentosum.jpg/500px-Piper_sarmentosum.jpg","w":500,"h":778,"by":"Sam P Mathew","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Piper_sarmentosum.jpg"}},"ingredient/mascarpone":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/mascarpone","n":"Mascarpone","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Mascarpone is a soft Italian dairy product obtained by acid-heat coagulation of cream. It is a dairy cream, not a cheese in the technical sense, as it is made without rennet and does not undergo true curd formation. It is recognized as a prodotto agroalimentare tradizionale (PAT)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-mascarpone"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mascarpone"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine",[["Tiramisu","dish/tiramisu"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Cocoa powder","ingredient/cocoa-powder"],["Coffee","ingredient/coffee"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Egg yolk","ingredient/egg-yolk"],["Espresso","ingredient/espresso"],["Ladyfinger (biscuit)","ingredient/ladyfinger-biscuit"],["Marsala wine","ingredient/marsala-wine"],["Savoiarde PAT","ingredient/savoiarde-pat"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/c/cd/Mascarpone_4.png/500px-Mascarpone_4.png","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Brodolò1","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mascarpone_4.png"}},"dish/u-pastizz-rtunnar":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/u-pastizz-rtunnar","n":"U' pastizz 'rtunnar","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Basilicatan (Lucanian) cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["U' pastizz 'rtunnar, commonly known as pastizz, is a baked turnover with a savoury filling, typical of the Basilicata region of Italy."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-u-pastizz-rtunnar"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U'_pastizz_'rtunnar"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Basilicatan (Lucanian) cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-basilicata"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"]]},{"